–Crippen molar refractivity (MR).\\ntype get_mol_mr = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return total ring count.\\ntype get_ring_count = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return aromatic ring count.\\ntype get_num_aromatic_rings = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return net formal charge (sum of atom formal charges as encoded in the SMILES).\\ntype get_formal_charge = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return QED (Quantitative Estimate of Drug-likeness) as implemented in RDKit.\\ntype get_qed = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return heteroatom count (non C/H).\\ntype get_num_heteroatoms = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Analyze fused ring systems (clusters of rings sharing bonds) and report their topology, aromaticity, and heteroatom content. Distinguishes fused polycyclic systems from isolated rings. Useful for AMES mutagenicity (detecting PAH-like systems), hERG (extended flat aromatic surfaces), and general structural classification of ring complexity.\\ntype analyze_ring_systems = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Classify the ionization state of a molecule at a target pH using Dimorphite-DL. Returns a formatted string with charge distributions.\\ntype classify_ionization = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Target pH for protonation (default: 7.4, physiological).\\nph?: number\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Compute fingerprint similarity (Morgan by default) between a query SMILES and a list of reference SMILES. Returns a formatted string.\\ntype compute_similarity = (_: {\\n// Query SMILES string.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// List of reference SMILES strings to compare against.\\nreference_smiles: string[]\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Screen a SMILES against RDKit\'s built-in structural alert catalogs. Returns a formatted string of alerts found.\\ntype score_structural_alerts = (_: {\\n// Query SMILES string.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Which alert library to use. \'all\' uses all available libraries.\\nalert_library?: "brenk" | "nih" | "chembl" | "zinc" | "all", // default: all\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Extract pharmacophore features using RDKit\'s BaseFeatures definitions.\\ntype extract_pharmacophore_features = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Accurately parse functional groups with attachment points, corresponding fragment SMILES, and atom ids in the SMILES string for better structure description.\\ntype describe_high_level_fg_fragments = (_: {\\n// The SMILES string to parse.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Test whether a molecule contains the given SMARTS substructures and count occurrences.\\ntype match_substructure = (_: {\\n// The SMILES string of the molecule. Do not pass in an ellipsis (`...`) or other abbreviation.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Mapping of pattern name to SMARTS string.\\npatterns: object\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Find the maximum common substructure (MCS) across query + reference SMILES. The output is a single structure present in ALL provided molecules. To compare very different molecules, call this tool multiple times individually rather than placing all molecules in a single list.\\ntype find_mcs = (_: {\\n// Query SMILES.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Reference SMILES to include in the MCS search.\\nreference_smiles: string[]\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Desalt a SMILES by removing salts/counterions and keeping ONLY the largest organic fragment. Use this tool when the SMILES contains multiple disconnected components separated by \'.\' and one or more components are likely inorganic ions/solvents (e.g., [Na+], Cl-, Br-, I-, [K+], [Li+], [Ca2+], [Mg2+], [NH4+], sulfate/phosphate, etc.), or when you want the parent (active) molecule for downstream property prediction.\\nIn aqueous/physiological conditions, salts typically dissociate; counterions are usually not part of the parent drug structure for many structure-based predictions.\\nTypical examples needing desalting: \'CC(=O)O.[Na+]\', \'[Na+].O=C(O)c1ccccc1\', \'CCO.Cl\'.\\nDo NOT use if the SMILES is already a single component (no \'.\') or if multiple organic fragments represent real mixture/co-crystal/combination therapy that should be kept. Output is the canonical SMILES of the retained parent fragment; smaller fragments are discarded.\\ntype remove_salts = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Screen a molecule against RDKit\'s built-in structural alert catalogs (PAINS, Brenk, NIH, ZINC, ChEMBL, etc.).\\ntype score_structural_alerts = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string,\\n// Alert library to screen against (default: all).\\nalert_library?: "all" | "pains" | "pains_a" | "pains_b" | "pains_c" | "brenk" | "nih" | "zinc" | "chembl" | "chembl_bms" | "chembl_lint" | "chembl_mlsmr"\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Labute approximate surface area (ASA proxy).\\ntype get_labute_asa = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return fraction of aromatic atoms.\\ntype get_fraction_aromatic_atoms = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return heterocycle count.\\ntype get_num_heterocycles = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return aromatic heterocycle count.\\ntype get_num_aromatic_heterocycles = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return atom stereocenter count.\\ntype get_num_atom_stereo_centers = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Bertz complexity index.\\ntype get_bertz_ct = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Kappa1 shape index.\\ntype get_kappa1 = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n// Return Kappa2 shape index.\\ntype get_kappa2 = (_: {\\n// SMILES string of the molecule.\\nsmiles: string\\n}) => any;\\n\\n} // namespace functionsuserInstructions: Answer the following question about drug properties.\\nContext: SARS-CoV-2 is a coronavirus that causes the COVID-19 disease and has led to millions of deaths worldwide within a few years. Discovering new drugs that can inhibit viral replication would be useful for combatting the virus. One method is to screen a library of small molecule drugs for binding to the SARS-CoV-2 3CL protease using X-ray crystallography.\\nQuestion: Given a drug SMILES string, predict whether it\\n(A) does not bind SARS-CoV-2 3CL protease (B) binds SARS-CoV-2 3CL protease\\nDrug SMILES: FC(F)(F)c1ccc(CN2CCOCC2)cc1Please think step by step and use tools when helpful. Then put your final choice ((A) or (B)) after "Answer:"assistantanalysisWe need to decide if a given drug binds SARS-CoV-2 3CL protease. Hard to predict. Without property info, can\'t determine. The question probably expects answer A or B uncertain. But maybe we could use cheminformatics: if drug has suitable binding motif like small aromatic with H-bond donor/acceptor, may bind protease. The compound is similar to known protease inhibitors (like nirmatrelvir). The SMILES FC(F)(F)c1ccc(CN2CCOCC2)cc1 shows a central aromatic ring with a tertiary amine side chain. This could potentially bind 3CL protease via pi stacking and H-bonding.\\n\\nWe can consider known 3CL protease inhibitors: e.g., GC376 (a cyclic dipeptide). Nirmatrelvir (a peptidomimetic). Our compound seems not a peptidomimetic but a small aromatic tertiary amine. Might not bind 3CL protease effectively.\\n\\nWithout experimental data, uncertain. But we could assume it does not bind (A). Because typical 3CL protease inhibitors are peptidomimetic, not simple aromatics. So likely does not bind. I\'ll answer (A).assistantfinalAnswer: (A)'
